<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Mongol invasion of Burma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1277">1277</a>–<a href="/years/1287">1287</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> was the series of campaigns from 1277 to 1287 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> against the Pagan Kingdom. After a battle at Ngasaunggyan (1277) and further invasions, Mongol forces reached the Pagan heartland in 1287; the kingdom broke apart after King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> was assassinated, ending more than two centuries of Pagan rule over the Irrawaddy valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-mongol-invasion-of-burma.jpg" alt="First Mongol invasion of Burma" width="320" height="634" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_invasions_of_Burma_(1277-87).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> reached the borders of Pagan in 1252 by invading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dali-kingdom">Dali Kingdom</a> to outflank Song China, taking its capital on 7 January 1253. Pagan had been in slow decline since the early 13th century: tax-free donations to religion had swallowed between one and two-thirds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a>'s cultivable land. In 1271-1272 and again in 1273 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> demanded a nominal tribute, part of his effort to cut off the retreat of Song refugees. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> refused both times, and from 1275 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> army occupied the contested borderlands, with leave to fight a limited border war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In April 1277 a Burmese army sent to recover the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of Gold Teeth was defeated at Ngasaunggyan, where the Mongol commander Huthukh had his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> dismount and shoot at the advancing elephants. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> again refused tribute in 1281, the emperor ordered an invasion of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>, though not of Pagan itself. The invasion began on 22 September 1283 under Prince Sangqudar; the fort at Ngasaunggyan fell on 3 December after a siege of more than two months, and Kaungsin six days later. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took Tagaung on 5 February 1284 and, after withdrawing in the heat, defeated another Burmese army near Hanlin on 26 January 1285.</p><p>The king fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wareru">Wareru</a> seized Martaban and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> revolted. A Burmese embassy led by the monk Shin Ditha Pamauk accepted Mongol suzerainty and an annual tribute at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in January 1287, in exchange for a withdrawal. On 1 July 1287 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> was assassinated by his second son, the Viceroy of Prome, and no ruler emerged who could honour the treaty. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> command then sent an army under Prince Ye-sin Timour south toward Pagan against Burmese defences led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athinkhaya">Athinkhaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazathingyan">Yazathingyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thihathu">Thihathu</a>; it may not have reached the city, and after heavy losses it fell back to Tagaung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> left the power vacuum unfilled, keeping northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> as the province of Zhengmian. Real power in central Burma passed to those three brothers, who overthrew King Kyawswa in December 1297, leading to the second Mongol invasion of 1300-1301. Historians see the war as hastening a collapse already under way, opening a long age of fragmentation and the rise of Tai-Shan states across mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The Burmese called the invaders Taruk, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narathihapate">Narathihapate</a> is remembered as Taruk-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pye-min">Pye Min</a>, the King who Fled from the Taruk.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Mongol invasion of Burma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
